  <Patch Id="VwFzkqTIudk3WFJ7NuLPzy">
    <Canvas Id="JmiFEp4QOOaaIqTCT5CxZR" DefaultCategory="Main" BordersChecked="false" CanvasType="FullCategory" />
    <!--

    ************************ Application ************************

-->
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="KjmcUgcOw7SoJDLDctltce">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <CategoryReference Kind="Category" Name="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="AH69EQMw4OCVeKbHgY4mWw">
        <Canvas Id="G5nIfOQBtacZrnoKjmQmjN" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="CFhuoLqSovHW1j1fdZItZC" Bounds="800,60,470,650" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="A round trip, and why it is the honest test&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This patch reads GeoJSON, writes it straight back out, and reads THAT. If both counts match, nothing was lost.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Write GeoJSON takes any sequence of features, so the spread out of Read GeoJSON connects, and so does one you built yourself. Note there is no FeatureCollection anywhere - it is a detail of the file format, and this package keeps it there.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Exact Round Trip - the pin worth understanding&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The standard says a polygon's outer ring winds counter-clockwise and its holes clockwise. With this pin OFF the writer rewinds your rings to obey that: the shape is unchanged, the coordinate ORDER is not. A polygon written and read back is then equal as a shape and unequal as a list of points - a maddening bug to chase. So the default is ON: write exactly what you have.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;What does NOT survive&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The SRID. GeoJSON has nowhere to put one - the format simply declares coordinates are longitude and latitude. Geometry projected into metres is written as if those metres were degrees, and nothing warns you. Convert before writing.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A GeoJSON id member arrives as an ordinary property called id, and goes back out as one.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Saving it to a file&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Write GeoJSON makes text; FileWriter (String) [IO] puts text on disk. Press SAVE and the GeoJSON out text is written to File Path. Nothing is written until you press it - a FileWriter whose Write pin were simply true would rewrite the file sixty times a second.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Then press READ IT BACK: FileReader reads the file, Read GeoJSON parses it, and the count on the right should match the one at the top. Two buttons on purpose - one press for both would race, and you could read the old file.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;written.geojson is a path RELATIVE TO THIS PATCH, so the file lands beside it. Type a full path to put it elsewhere. Never leave File Path empty: an empty path is not nothing, it means this patch's own folder.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="EmJvvsYsTmMzB6I0gP6hdy" Comment="GeoJSON in - edit me" Bounds="62,51,540,80" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="{&quot;type&quot;:&quot;FeatureCollection&quot;,&quot;features&quot;:[{&quot;type&quot;:&quot;Feature&quot;,&quot;geometry&quot;:{&quot;type&quot;:&quot;Point&quot;,&quot;coordinates&quot;:[139.6917,35.6895]},&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Tokyo&quot;,&quot;population&quot;:13960000}},{&quot;type&quot;:&quot;Feature&quot;,&quot;geometry&quot;:{&quot;type&quot;:&quot;Polygon&quot;,&quot;coordinates&quot;:[[[0,0],[4,0],[4,4],[0,4],[0,0]],[[1,1],[1,2],[2,2],[2,1],[1,1]]]},&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;a square with a hole&quot;}}]}">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
          </Pad>
